{
  "rules": [
    "Persistent positive bias: forecasts are consistently too high versus realized prices (often by ~5–15 EUR), especially during downtrends. Apply a downward level shift to the whole path when recent momentum is negative.",
    "Trend/turning-point lag: the model fails to capture sharp sell-offs and keeps pricing near the prior regime. If the last 5–10 days show consecutive declines, steepen the forecasted slope downward (increase negative drift) rather than flatlining.",
    "Insufficient downside convexity: large drops are underpredicted (true path falls faster and further). Add a nonlinear correction where larger recent drawdowns imply disproportionately larger additional forecast cuts.",
    "Overshoot after local highs/spikes: when history is near multi-week highs (e.g., >~80), the model projects stabilization/rebound, but outcomes mean-revert lower. If spot is in the top quartile of the last 30 days, enforce mean reversion toward the 30d average (or below) over the next 2–3 weeks.",
    "Horizon-specific error growth: bias increases with horizon (later days are more overestimated than early days). Apply an increasing downward adjustment with lead time (e.g., small cut days 1–5, larger cut days 15–30).",
    "Volatility mismatch: forecast paths are too smooth relative to realized declines. When recent realized volatility is elevated, widen downside moves (allow larger day-to-day negative steps) rather than keeping a narrow band."
  ]
}
